      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1890s Richmond buzzed with a post office, Methodist church, general store, and an Independent Order of Odd Fellows hall.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/richmond-oregon-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Richmond’s post office operated from 1899 to 1952, marking its rise and eventual abandonment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richmond%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Sheepshooters’ War and declining mining profits left Richmond isolated, leading to its abandonment in the early 1900s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        23,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/richmond-oregon-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today, the standing Methodist church and IOOF hall still echo Richmond’s once‑bustling frontier community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/richmond-oregon-ghost-town/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
